<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The agent's execution plane, outside the agent's process tree.

  Founder, 2026-09-13: "the agent shouldn't have raw, unrestricted bash spawned directly from its
  own process tree anyway. It should request execution from an isolated daemon." This is that
  daemon's launchd job.

  WHY IT IS SAFE TO LEAVE RUNNING, and why that is a design choice rather than an oversight:
  a port raised the cost to this machine's disk (measured 18.06 GiB, 5.6 GiB free) and the number
  the job service actually has is 60. So the daemon listens on a UNIX domain socket, is O(n) in the
  command, and costs nothing while idle: launchd starts it, and it blocks on accept().

  RunAtLoad brings it back after a reboot. KeepAlive restarts it if it dies, which is correct here
  and was correct to omit from ai.estate.idp: this process does NOT fork and exit; it serves, so
  a non-zero exit really is a death.

  What makes the boundary real is ownership: the agent must not be able to edit this file or the
  daemon it names. It runs as the same uid today, so this is the half that can be built now, and
  `bin/idp-executor-status` reports which half is actually in place rather than leaving a
  half-boundary to read as a whole one.

  Install:    bin/idp-executor-install
  Remove:     launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/ai.estate.executor
  Check:      bin/idp-executor-status
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>${PATH}</string>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>${HOME}</string>
	</dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>ai.estate.executor</string>
	<key>LowPriorityIO</key>
	<true/>
	<key>Nice</key>
	<integer>5</integer>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>${IDP}/bin/exec-daemon</string>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- This process serves rather than forks, so a death is a death. -->
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>10</integer>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>${HOME}/.estate/executor.out.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>${HOME}/.estate/executor.err.log</string>
	<!-- The execution plane never needs a shell of its own: it is handed a command and it bounds
	     that command. Launching it in a login shell would give it exactly the surface this
	     design exists to remove. -->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Interactive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
